# Club, slug, title, deck, player IDs, selected game IDs, schedule IDs, log IDs, manuscript.
BEATS = [
    (6, "charlotte-lead-after-miami", "Charlotte’s lead survived four close losses",
     "The Colonels banked just enough on the first two legs of their road trip to survive a four-game Miami sweep.",
     [5456, 7208, 1972], [1454, 1466, 1478, 1490, 1501, 1508, 1525, 1537, 1549, 1561], [1581, 1593, 1605, 1814, 1826, 1838, 1850], [1561], [
        "Six runs separated Charlotte from Miami across four games. The Colonels lost 4–2, 4–3, 5–3 and 6–5. On August 30 they had the game tied in the bottom of the ninth before Pat Watters ended it with a single. The 1980 division winner heads home still first, with its lead down to a game and a half.",
        "Tampa Bay and Atlanta explain why Charlotte still has that lead. The Colonels won four of their first six, adding a game to their August 19 margin before the sweep. In Miami, Dabir Aly struck out ten in the opener and the Colonels scored twice. Miguel Salazar had 17 hits over the full challenge. There was individual production on a trip where four short scores in succession mattered more.",
        "Charlotte opens September at home against Chicago, itself a game out in the Central. Miami comes to Charlotte for four September 24–27. Those dates put the advantage back in the Colonels’ hands if they can carry a lead to the rematch.",
    ]),
    (1, "miami-makes-four-count", "Miami made all four count",
     "The Hurricanes left Atlanta in a worse position, then turned the Charlotte series into the league’s most productive four nights for a chaser.",
     [1849, 1972, 9405], [1453, 1465, 1477, 1489, 1509, 1520, 1525, 1537, 1549, 1561], [1575, 1584, 1596, 1814, 1826, 1838, 1850], [1561], [
        "The last out of the Charlotte series never came. Pat Watters singled with two gone in the ninth, Dwayne Postell scored, and Miami won its fourth straight game against the division leader. On August 20 the question was whether Miami could get close enough that four wins would give it first. Six games later the answer was still no. The Hurricanes needed those four just to get within a game and a half.",
        "Atlanta took three of the opening four. Two Miami wins over Tampa Bay followed, by 10–3 and 9–1. Then Addie de Ruijter, acquired from Phoenix on July 29, drove in all four runs in the first game against Charlotte. The newcomers were part of the August 20 question. De Ruijter gave Miami a concrete answer in a game with a direct bearing on first place.",
        "Miami now owns the NBC wild-card lead by half a game over Chicago. At 71 wins it is within two of its entire 1980 total. Its next series is in Phoenix, where de Ruijter’s former club leads the West. The return series with Charlotte begins September 24 on the Colonels’ field.",
    ]),
    (3, "atlanta-made-the-east-work", "Atlanta made the East leaders earn it",
     "The Kings shaped the Charlotte–Miami margin, then swept Tampa Bay behind a productive stretch from Jordan Santistevan.",
     [1054, 4545, 7392, 7613], [1453, 1465, 1477, 1489, 1501, 1508, 1526, 1538, 1550, 1562], [1573, 1582, 1594], [], [
        "Before Charlotte ever got to Miami, Atlanta had a vote. The Kings took three of four from the Hurricanes and split two with Charlotte, opening the latter set with a 9–3 win. Miami entered its showdown five and a half back. Atlanta mattered to that margin even though it finished August 30 ten games out.",
        "The Kings swept the closing set with Tampa Bay, including a 14-inning 8–6 win. Eric Gray had five hits in the 10–0 opener, and Jordan Santistevan drove in three runs in each of the first two. Across the ten games Atlanta outscored the opposition 61–28. It took a good stretch against the leaders and turned it into an 8–2 challenge.",
        "Dallas gets the Kings next. The Rustlers have a one-game lead over idle Chicago on August 31. Atlanta’s first outside assignment gives it another chance to leave its mark on somebody else’s race.",
    ]),
    (8, "tampa-bay-kimpton-kept-reaching", "Tampa Bay kept finding Walter Kimpton on base",
     "A rare win over Charlotte and Kimpton’s work getting on and moving up give the Storm concrete September questions after a 1–9 stretch.",
     [7350, 1005, 4358], [1454, 1466, 1478, 1490, 1509, 1520, 1526, 1538, 1550, 1562], [1574, 1583, 1595], [], [
        "Nate Smeltz drove in five on August 23, and the Storm needed them in a 10–9 win over Charlotte. It was the club’s only win of the challenge. Tampa Bay later took Atlanta to the 14th and lost 8–6. The two games are worth keeping separate from the 81 runs the Storm allowed across the ten-game stretch.",
        "Walter Kimpton had 12 hits, ten walks and six stolen bases in the challenge. The Storm scored only four runs in two games at Miami and were shut out in their first game back home. Kimpton kept giving the batters behind him a chance to change that pattern.",
        "The Storm go to Los Angeles next. With the expansion date approaching, keep Kimpton’s trips on base in view and watch which hitters get the chances to move him. There is a September job to define even with Tampa Bay 19 games out.",
    ]),
    (4, "dallas-sweep-does-not-buy-breathing-room", "Dallas swept the champions and still has Chicago behind it",
     "Anton Garay’s eight scoreless innings helped Dallas win a series it needed just to retain a one-game lead.",
     [5873, 6196, 2859], [1456, 1468, 1480, 1492, 1502, 1510, 1527, 1539, 1551, 1563], [1573, 1789, 1798, 1899, 1911, 1923, 1935], [], [
        "Dallas opened the challenge with 17 runs at Minneapolis and lost the next three there. It split two games in Chicago and was level with the Fire when Detroit arrived. The Rustlers’ four-game sweep of the defending champions was emphatic, by a combined 20–4. The reward in the standings was a one-game lead, with Chicago still close enough to make the next Dallas loss count.",
        "Anton Garay allowed four hits over eight scoreless innings against Detroit on August 29. He had already given Dallas eight scoreless innings in Minneapolis with no decision, in a game the Rustlers lost 1–0 in ten. The two starts tell the difference between getting a good outing and turning it into an August result.",
        "Dallas opens at home against Atlanta, an 8–2 club in the challenge. Six games remain against the Fire. Two are in Chicago in September, and the final four of the season are in Dallas.",
    ]),
    (12, "chicago-earned-the-right-to-chase", "Chicago earned the right to chase Dallas",
     "A 7–3 stretch turned the Fire’s August case from run differential into actual wins, without settling either route to October.",
     [8125, 8025, 854, 11346], [1455, 1467, 1479, 1491, 1502, 1510, 1528, 1540, 1552, 1564], [1581, 1593, 1605, 1789, 1899, 1935], [1540], [
        "The August 20 Observer conversation gave Chicago credit for a plus-27 run differential in the Conference Challenges and asked when it would show in the standings. This time seven wins came with a much smaller surplus, 40 runs to 34. Heriberto Jimenez gave the Fire eight scoreless innings and 13 strikeouts in their 1–0 opening victory over Detroit. Chicago won both games on August 21 and took three of four from the Dukes.",
        "The Fire split with Dallas and lost a 14-inning game at Minneapolis on Hirotoshi Nishimoto’s sacrifice fly. They took the other three games from the Blizzard. Those are the wins that mattered while Dallas was sweeping Detroit. Last year Chicago won this division and Detroit went on to win the Grand Series. Chicago now has to find a way past Dallas or Miami.",
        "The Fire start September at Charlotte. Playing the East leader while Miami opens in Phoenix tests both of Chicago’s routes. Dallas remains on the schedule six times, four of them on the final weekend.",
    ]),
    (5, "detroit-runs-short-on-the-road", "Detroit’s title defense reaches San Francisco six back",
     "The Dukes entered one game off Dallas and in the wild-card spot. Four runs in four games at Dallas left both routes steeper.",
     [4648, 1857, 4963], [1455, 1467, 1479, 1491, 1511, 1521, 1527, 1539, 1551, 1563], [1576, 1586, 1598, 1610, 1622, 1634], [], [
        "Detroit scored 11 in its only win of the challenge and four runs in four games at Dallas. The 11–3 win in Chicago came after two August 21 losses. Minneapolis then won twice in Detroit before the Dukes went to Dallas. A team that had begun the challenge one game out ended it six back.",
        "Alex Diaz had 14 hits and six walks over the ten games. Against Dallas, Detroit never scored more than two in a game. Both facts are worth carrying into September. A productive line from one hitter did not turn those four games, and the champions also lost their place at the top of the wild-card standings.",
        "Detroit opens at San Francisco, a club two places ahead of it on the wild-card line behind Miami, with Los Angeles also between them. The next stop is Charlotte. Both opponents have races of their own to protect, and Detroit needs to start taking decisions from teams in front of it.",
    ]),
    (11, "minneapolis-one-run-games", "Minneapolis made three one-run games count",
     "The Blizzard took games from both Central leaders and the defending champions, with Jose Varela giving them a chance in two extra-inning wins.",
     [2756, 11346, 6956, 12030], [1456, 1468, 1480, 1492, 1511, 1521, 1528, 1540, 1552, 1564], [1585, 1597, 1606], [1540], [
        "A 17–3 loss on August 20 could have set the terms for Dallas’s four days in Minneapolis. The Blizzard won the next three. Jose Varela worked eight scoreless in the 1–0 win in ten innings, and Minneapolis put ten runs on Dallas the next day. It went on to win twice in Detroit, once in 13 innings.",
        "Varela pitched eight again August 28, this time allowing two earned runs against Chicago. The score was still tied in the 14th when Hirotoshi Nishimoto’s fly brought Ricky Thompson home. That was the Blizzard’s third one-run win of the challenge. Six wins left them the same eleven and a half games behind Dallas. They mattered to the opponents who had to count them.",
        "Minneapolis goes to San Diego on September 1 with 32 games still listed. Follow the rotation through the opening series, with Varela’s two long starts giving the next set of outings a benchmark.",
    ]),
    (2, "phoenix-started-by-winning-in-san-francisco", "Phoenix took the lead on San Francisco’s field",
     "Tony Zacate’s eight scoreless innings ended the opening series, and his August 30 start kept the lead intact.",
     [1440, 2025], [1458, 1470, 1482, 1494, 1503, 1512, 1529, 1541, 1553, 1565], [1575, 1584, 1596, 1866, 1878, 1890], [], [
        "Two clubs arrived at the opening series on 64–57. Phoenix won the first two in San Francisco, dropped the third 2–1, then closed the set with a 2–0 win. Tony Zacate went eight scoreless and allowed two hits in that last game. The Firebirds finished the challenge one game in front. The two-game margin they took out of San Francisco bought them time when the Warriors won their closing series.",
        "Phoenix split with Los Angeles and split at San Diego. Zacate returned for the August 30 4–1 win, allowing one earned run in 7⅔ innings. Nick Perez had 17 hits and four steals over the stretch. The team that won 107 games and this division last season has a much smaller margin to protect this time.",
        "The Firebirds open the next series at home against Miami, which arrives with six straight wins. They return to San Francisco for three September 28–30. The first meeting showed what Phoenix could do in that park. The second may ask it to protect what it won there.",
    ]),
    (10, "san-francisco-foothold-against-los-angeles", "San Francisco found a foothold against Los Angeles",
     "The Warriors lost the opening duel with Phoenix but took three of four from Los Angeles, with Bernardo Valenzuela helping close the series.",
     [8897, 5838, 5575], [1458, 1470, 1482, 1494, 1504, 1513, 1530, 1542, 1554, 1566], [1576, 1586, 1598, 1866, 1878, 1890], [], [
        "The August 20 forecast called for a San Francisco recovery against familiar opponents. Phoenix took three of four in the Warriors’ own park. San Francisco then split two at San Diego. The recovery finally found a place to start in the four with Los Angeles, and the Warriors ended the challenge one game out instead of looking up at both rivals.",
        "San Francisco put 15 runs on Los Angeles in the opener and won the last two. Bernardo Valenzuela homered and drove in three in the 10–4 finale. Miguel Collado hit four home runs over the full challenge. The offense that scored five runs in its last three games before the Los Angeles series scored 34 in the four against the Cobras.",
        "Detroit comes to San Francisco on August 31. A Warriors win and a Phoenix loss would tie the West again. The next Phoenix series waits until September 28, also on the Warriors’ field.",
    ]),
    (9, "los-angeles-fourteen-innings-but-no-ground", "The Cobras won 14 innings and gained no ground on Phoenix",
     "Los Angeles had a 5–0 start and a six-win challenge, but a losing series at San Francisco kept it two games back.",
     [4584, 4928, 5405, 1956], [1457, 1469, 1481, 1493, 1503, 1512, 1530, 1542, 1554, 1566], [1574, 1583, 1595, 1800, 1810, 1901, 1937], [], [
        "Fernando Domenech had four hits in the 14-inning win that completed Los Angeles’s sweep of San Diego. The Cobras scored ten against Phoenix next. Five straight wins were enough to get their attention in the West. Over the last five, only the 7–3 win in San Francisco went their way.",
        "Ramon Reyes homered twice in the finale against the Warriors. Los Angeles still lost 10–4 and finished two back of Phoenix, the same gap it had on August 19. A six-win challenge left a clear September assignment. There are runs to be had, but the opening sweep cannot stand in for wins against the two teams ahead.",
        "The Cobras host Tampa Bay next and have six games left against Phoenix. Four are in Phoenix to end the season. San Francisco visits Los Angeles for four in September before those final games arrive.",
    ]),
    (7, "san-diego-what-two-wins-took", "San Diego found a way to take two from first place",
     "Two wins against Phoenix after a Los Angeles sweep make the Seraphs’ starting staff worth following into roster expansion.",
     [10161, 9599, 3555, 4595], [1457, 1469, 1481, 1493, 1504, 1513, 1529, 1541, 1553, 1565], [1585, 1597, 1606], [], [
        "The Seraphs had one run in the two middle games at Los Angeles and were swept, though the last loss took 14 innings. At home they beat San Francisco 4–3 and took consecutive games from Phoenix, 3–1 and 5–3. Those two wins took two days out of the Firebirds’ chance to extend their West lead.",
        "Barbu Nica let in one earned run over 14⅓ innings in two starts during the challenge. Elijah Lundy gave the Seraphs 14⅔ innings and allowed three earned runs in his two. There is more to learn from those outings than from the club’s 48–83 record alone, especially when the offense supplied only 23 runs in ten games.",
        "Minneapolis visits September 1. With roster expansion scheduled that day, the immediate question is how San Diego uses the innings around its starters, and whether the extra places turn into real work on the field.",
    ]),
    (20, "boston-galvez-bought-room", "Jorge Galvez gave Boston room to breathe",
     "The Patriots withstood Philadelphia’s winning streak by allowing little in their own wins, culminating in Galvez’s five-hit shutout.",
     [1, 3081], [1459, 1471, 1483, 1495, 1514, 1522, 1532, 1544, 1556, 1568], [1588, 1600, 1607, 1867, 1879, 1891], [], [
        "Philadelphia came to Boston for four with a four-game deficit. The Patriots shut the Fury out twice, then lost the next two by a combined 18–4. The split sent the Fury away still four back. Boston kept three of those games in hand even after Philadelphia ran its winning streak to eight.",
        "Jorge Galvez allowed one earned run in 24⅓ innings during the challenge. On August 30 in Pittsburgh he gave up five hits over nine innings and Boston needed only two runs. Mike Randolph recorded six saves in the challenge. The finale showed that Boston could finish a series without calling on him.",
        "Boston won seven of ten and starts September at Houston. The Fury return to Boston on September 28. The Patriots have several road series before then in which to protect the margin their pitchers helped keep.",
    ]),
    (22, "philadelphia-eight-straight", "Eight straight wins brought Philadelphia back within three",
     "Ron Stout’s 21 hits helped turn two shutout losses into an eight-game run. Boston still has the lead and hosts the last meeting.",
     [3201, 5140, 4859], [1459, 1471, 1483, 1495, 1505, 1515, 1531, 1543, 1555, 1567], [1590, 1602, 1608, 1867, 1879, 1891], [], [
        "Philadelphia went 18 innings without a run to start the challenge. It scored 18 over the next two games at Boston. The two wins restored the four-game division gap and began a streak that ran through Pittsburgh and all four games with New York.",
        "Ron Stout had 21 hits and 15 RBI in the ten games. Four of those hits came in the 10–0 finale against New York, when Gilberto Flores worked 7⅔ scoreless innings. The Fury allowed four runs in the whole New York series. Boston kept winning too, so eight straight took only one game off the deficit from August 19.",
        "Philadelphia starts September in Las Vegas, while Boston goes to Houston. The three remaining head-to-head games are at Boston September 28–30. Three back today is close enough to give that series weight, with two different West and Central tests to get through first.",
    ]),
    (21, "pittsburgh-good-first-reference-point", "Joe Good gave Pittsburgh a start it can build on",
     "A trade acquisition kept the Express close against Boston. Three early wins over New York did not carry over against the East’s top two.",
     [3032, 6620, 965], [1460, 1472, 1484, 1496, 1505, 1515, 1532, 1544, 1556, 1568], [1578, 1589, 1601], [], [
        "Joe Good struck out six and allowed one earned run in 5⅓ innings on August 30. The Express lost 2–0 because Jorge Galvez shut them out. For Pittsburgh, the line is a useful entry in the record of what it received from Miami in the July 28 deal that sent Daniel Vega the other way.",
        "Pittsburgh answered the opening 12–8 loss to New York with three straight wins, two by a run. It went 1–5 the rest of the way against Philadelphia and Boston. The two games in Philadelphia and the Boston finale were all decided by two runs or fewer. Last year’s East winner finished the challenge ten back, with a sharper question about where the offense comes from when a starter gives it that kind of game.",
        "The Denver series begins August 31. Good’s Boston start is evidence for following his next outing as September opens, while the offense has a chance to recover against a club on the wild-card line.",
    ]),
    (15, "new-york-from-twelve-to-zero", "New York went from twelve runs to two shutouts",
     "The Aces won their opener and lost nine straight. Robby Chacon’s work at the plate gives their Cincinnati series a narrower question than the record.",
     [2144, 8707], [1460, 1472, 1484, 1496, 1514, 1522, 1531, 1543, 1555, 1567], [1577, 1587, 1599], [], [
        "The Aces scored 12 in Pittsburgh on August 20. They scored 14 in the nine games that followed and lost every one. In Philadelphia they were shut out twice to end the challenge. The last of those games, 10–0, told a different story from two earlier one-run losses at Pittsburgh, when a single extra run might have changed the result.",
        "Robby Chacon hit safely 11 times in 35 at-bats and drew six walks during the challenge. That is one usable part of a lineup that managed four runs in four games at Philadelphia. It gives the Aces something more specific to examine than the 1–9 record.",
        "New York returns home to face Cincinnati on August 31. With expansion scheduled one day later, a useful watch is whether the Aces change who hits around the players already reaching base. The first new lineup will tell more than an assumption about who is coming up.",
    ]),
    (23, "houston-eight-game-buffer", "Houston still has eight games to spend",
     "Five losses to St. Louis made Houston’s lead look less comfortable. Two late wins over Nashville kept it substantial.",
     [2383, 8032], [1113, 1462, 1474, 1486, 1498, 1506, 1516, 1533, 1545, 1557, 1569], [1588, 1600, 1607, 1620, 1632, 1644, 1870, 1905, 1941], [], [
        "Houston lost twice to St. Louis on August 20, on different fields. First came the 5–3 completion of the unfinished game in Houston, then a 7–1 loss in St. Louis to open the regular four-game set. Houston lost those four by a combined 32–3. A stretch that began with the league’s best record ended with eight losses in 11 games.",
        "There were ways for Houston to win even in that stretch. Scott Szell allowed three earned runs in 25 innings across three starts. The Mavericks beat Cincinnati 3–2 in 16 innings, and their bats recovered for 10–0 and 10–9 wins to finish against Nashville. Apostolos Georghiou homered and had three hits in the last game. Houston still leads Cincinnati and St. Louis by eight.",
        "Boston and Denver come to Houston to start September. The East leader and one of the two clubs tied for the wild card give Houston an early look at the company it may have in October.",
    ]),
    (19, "st-louis-made-up-six-games", "St. Louis made up six games and earned another look",
     "The Stallions went 9–2, beat Houston five times and moved level with Cincinnati behind Danny Martinez and Chris Jorge.",
     [8217, 6418, 6733], [1113, 1462, 1474, 1486, 1498, 1517, 1523, 1534, 1546, 1558, 1570], [1579, 1591, 1603, 1821, 1833, 1845, 1857, 1870, 1882, 1894], [], [
        "St. Louis won five games against Houston before the two clubs were done with their opening assignment. One was the completion of the unfinished game in Houston. The other four came at home. Chris Jorge threw a six-hit shutout in the 9–0 finale. Danny Martinez had seven hits over the two shutout wins that ended that set.",
        "The Stallions won three of four from Cincinnati, too. In the 4–2 finale Bert Lopez struck out eight in 7⅔ innings. An 11-game challenge produced nine wins, 77 runs and the same 67–64 record as Cincinnati. St. Louis already has five more wins than it managed in all of 1980. It is eight behind Houston and four off the wild-card line.",
        "The Stallions go to Portland on August 31. Houston visits again September 28–30, immediately after four at Cincinnati. That run will test whether the gains St. Louis made playing those clubs in August can survive the rest of its schedule.",
    ]),
    (14, "cincinnati-one-gain-one-new-problem", "Cincinnati gained on Houston and picked up a rival",
     "Alex Perez earned a split with Houston. The Cougars still finished tied with St. Louis after losing three games there.",
     [7584, 7393], [1461, 1473, 1485, 1497, 1506, 1516, 1534, 1546, 1558, 1570], [1577, 1587, 1599, 1821, 1833, 1845, 1857, 1905, 1941], [], [
        "Cincinnati lost to Houston in 16 innings August 24. The next day Alex Perez struck out ten and held the Mavericks scoreless for 8⅓ innings in a 4–0 win. The split was useful. The Cougars gained a game and a half on Houston over the challenge even though they went 4–6.",
        "The trip to St. Louis shows why that progress has limits. The Cougars scored one run in the first two games combined. Jordan Pedroza drove in four in their 6–5 win on August 29. St. Louis won the last game 4–2, leaving both clubs 67–64, eight back of Houston and four off the wild-card line.",
        "Cincinnati opens at New York. Before taking four more games against Houston on the final weekend, it gets another four with St. Louis at home September 24–27. A good start away from the division would give both of those series more room to matter.",
    ]),
    (16, "nashville-even-with-everyone", "Nashville split with every Central opponent",
     "Jose Orozco drove in 14 as the Blues played every series to a draw, including two wins in Houston.",
     [9363, 1057, 6643], [1461, 1473, 1485, 1497, 1517, 1523, 1533, 1545, 1557, 1569], [1580, 1592, 1604], [1569], [
        "Nashville won twice at Cincinnati and split two with St. Louis. Then the Blues won the first two in Houston and lost the last two. The potential go-ahead run was on second when Juan Grant lined out to center for the last out of the 10–9 finale.",
        "Jose Orozco drove in 14 over the challenge and Brett Petrillo drove in 12. Nashville scored 60 runs. It finished 5–5 and eleven games back, with two wins against the division leader and three winnable series that stayed even.",
        "The schedule sends the Blues to Seattle next. Their lineup will have another say in a pennant race when the Comets start the series level with Denver for the wild card.",
    ]),
    (17, "las-vegas-one-game-left-of-cushion", "Las Vegas brought one game of its lead home",
     "A 3–7 road trip nearly erased the Gamblers’ cushion until Rogelio Cole helped win the finale at Portland.",
     [6052, 8713], [1464, 1476, 1488, 1500, 1507, 1518, 1535, 1547, 1559, 1571], [1590, 1602, 1608, 1806, 1812, 1872, 1884, 1896, 1907, 1943], [], [
        "Las Vegas left for Seattle three games ahead of Denver and five ahead of Seattle. The Comets took the first three, and Denver won both games in the middle series. When Las Vegas lost 6–5 in Portland on August 29, Seattle was level with it in the standings.",
        "Rogelio Cole worked 7⅔ innings and allowed two runs in the 5–2 win that ended the trip. Denver’s win at Seattle left both challengers a game behind. Las Vegas kept first after a 3–7 stretch on a schedule that gave both pursuers direct chances to take it away.",
        "Philadelphia arrives September 1 on eight straight wins. The Gamblers still owe Denver six games and Seattle three. Home field against Denver to close the season will matter more if Las Vegas can make something of the games before those nine.",
    ]),
    (13, "denver-earned-both-routes", "Denver put the division within reach",
     "Two wins over Las Vegas and two at the end of the Seattle series left Denver a game from first and tied for the wild card.",
     [949, 1733, 6906], [1463, 1475, 1487, 1499, 1507, 1518, 1536, 1548, 1560, 1572], [1578, 1589, 1601, 1620, 1823, 1835, 1847, 1859, 1907, 1943], [], [
        "Portland took the first three in Denver. The Rocketeers won the fourth, swept two from Las Vegas, then lost the first two in Seattle by a run each. That left Denver two games behind Seattle going into August 29, with two more games on the same field.",
        "Tony Oubre went eight innings in the 4–2 win. Damian Serano struck out seven in 5⅔ innings in the 2–1 finale. Denver came home level with Seattle and one game behind Las Vegas. The 5–5 record matters less than the clubs Denver took those four late decisions from.",
        "Pittsburgh arrives first and Denver goes to Houston next. Four more with Seattle are in Denver on September 24–27. The schedule still gives the Rocketeers a chance to turn a tie for one berth into control of a division.",
    ]),
    (24, "seattle-what-a-one-run-game-costs", "Seattle saw both sides of a one-run finish",
     "Adam Wilson ended a 14-inning win over Las Vegas. A one-run loss to Denver on August 30 left the Comets tied for the wild card.",
     [1952, 8049, 6735, 6611], [1464, 1476, 1488, 1500, 1519, 1524, 1536, 1548, 1560, 1572], [1580, 1592, 1604, 1823, 1859, 1872, 1884, 1896], [1476], [
        "Adam Wilson singled in the 14th inning August 21, scoring David Olguin to beat Las Vegas 4–3. Seattle took three of four from the Gamblers and both games at Portland. It shared first before the final day. Then Denver won the finale 2–1, and Las Vegas finished its own trip with a win.",
        "Tony Miller struck out eight in 7⅓ innings in that last loss. Carlos Merlos had 17 hits during the challenge. Seattle went 7–3 and made up four games on Las Vegas. Losing the last two let Denver catch it.",
        "Nashville comes in on August 31. Denver and Seattle share the wild-card line at 71–60, and Las Vegas still has to come back to Seattle for three September 28–30. The Comets get time to get ahead before the next couple of one-run games carry the same kind of price.",
    ]),
    (18, "portland-campbell-and-the-contenders", "Jeremy Campbell gave the West something to work around",
     "Seven home runs, including three in two wins over Las Vegas, helped Portland leave a mark on both sides of the race.",
     [2955, 1815], [1463, 1475, 1487, 1499, 1519, 1524, 1535, 1547, 1559, 1571], [1579, 1591, 1603, 1944], [], [
        "Portland won three straight in Denver to open the challenge. Then it lost both games to Seattle, took two from Las Vegas and finished 5–5. Eighteen games out, the Lumberjacks still made each of the three West contenders account for them.",
        "Jeremy Campbell hit seven homers and drove in 13 in those ten games. He went deep once in Portland’s 5–3 win against Las Vegas and twice in the 6–5 win the following day. Frank Jahr saved four games during the challenge without an earned run. The Lumberjacks had scoring and late innings worth following even when they could make little immediate use of the wins themselves.",
        "St. Louis arrives just off a 9–2 stretch. Portland has a chance to find out whether Campbell’s August work carries into another series against a club with something at stake.",
    ]),
]
